/*!
\group cmake-commands-qtbuildtools
\title CMake Commands in Qt6 BuildTools

The following CMake commands are defined when Qt6::BuildTools is loaded, for instance
with

\code
find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS BuildTools)
\endcode

\sa{CMake Command Reference}
*/

/*!
\page qttools-cmake-qt-mangle-objc-symbols.html
\ingroup cmake-commands-qtbuildtools

\title qt_mangle_objc_symbols
\keyword qt_mangle_objc_symbols()

\summary {Mangles Objective-C class and category names in a target's binary.}

The command is defined in the \c BuildTools component of the \c Qt6 package.
Load the package with:

\code
find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS BuildTools)
\endcode

\cmakecommandsince 6.12
\preliminarycmakecommand

\section1 Synopsis

\badcode
qt_mangle_objc_symbols(target
    (OLD_NAMESPACE namespace | USE_QT_NAMESPACE_AS_OLD)
    (NEW_NAMESPACE replacement | GENERATE_RANDOM_NEW_NAMESPACE)
    [EXCLUDE_CLASSES class...]
    [QUIET]
    [CODESIGN]
    [CODESIGN_IDENTITY identity]
)
\endcode

\versionlessCMakeCommandsNote qt6_mangle_objc_symbols()

\section1 Description

Mangles Objective-C class and category names in the \c target binary by
replacing the specified namespace with either a provided replacement string
or a unique random string of the same length.

This is useful when statically linking multiple libraries that each embed
their own copy of the Objective-C runtime information for the same class
names, which would otherwise clash at load time.

This command adds a \c POST_BUILD step to \c target. Note that this step
potentially invalidates code signatures, so custom codesigning needs to be
installed after the name mangling (for example, using the \c CODESIGN
option). The \c POST_BUILD step:

\list 1
    \li Mangles Objective-C symbols by replacing the namespace.
    \li Optionally re-signs the binary if \c CODESIGN is specified.
\endlist

The replacement string must be the same length as the original namespace for
binary safety. When \c GENERATE_RANDOM_NEW_NAMESPACE is used, a random string
of the same length is automatically generated. When using
\c USE_QT_NAMESPACE_AS_OLD with \c NEW_NAMESPACE, if the replacement is
shorter than the Qt namespace, it will be padded with underscores; if longer,
a warning will be issued and the replacement will be truncated.

Objective-C symbol mangling is only supported on Apple platforms. On other
platforms, this command issues a warning (unless \c QUIET is given) and does
nothing.

\section1 Arguments

\c target is the target whose binary will be patched.

\c OLD_NAMESPACE is the namespace prefix to replace, for example
\c{"AcmeApp"} or \c{"MyNamespace"}.

\c USE_QT_NAMESPACE_AS_OLD uses the Qt namespace read from
\c{Qt::Core}'s \c QT_NAMESPACE property instead of an explicit
\c OLD_NAMESPACE.

\c NEW_NAMESPACE is the replacement string. With \c OLD_NAMESPACE, it must be
the same length as \c OLD_NAMESPACE. With \c USE_QT_NAMESPACE_AS_OLD, it will
be padded with underscores if shorter, or truncated with a warning if
longer.

\c GENERATE_RANDOM_NEW_NAMESPACE generates a random replacement string
instead of an explicit \c NEW_NAMESPACE.

\c EXCLUDE_CLASSES lists class names to exclude from mangling. May be
specified multiple times.

\c QUIET suppresses output from the mangler tool.

\c CODESIGN re-signs the binary after mangling (macOS only).

\c CODESIGN_IDENTITY sets the code signing identity to use. Defaults to
\c{"-"} for ad-hoc signing.

\section1 Examples

With an explicit replacement:

\badcode
qt_mangle_objc_symbols(MyApp
    OLD_NAMESPACE "AcmeApp"
    NEW_NAMESPACE "ACMEAPP"
    CODESIGN
)
\endcode

With a random namespace:

\badcode
qt_mangle_objc_symbols(MyApp
    OLD_NAMESPACE "AcmeApp"
    GENERATE_RANDOM_NEW_NAMESPACE
    CODESIGN
)
\endcode

With excluded classes:

\badcode
qt_mangle_objc_symbols(MyApp
    OLD_NAMESPACE "AcmeApp"
    NEW_NAMESPACE "ACMEAPP"
    EXCLUDE_CLASSES AcmeApp_Internal AcmeApp_Private
    CODESIGN
)
\endcode

Using the Qt namespace from \c{Qt::Core}:

\badcode
qt_mangle_objc_symbols(MyApp
    USE_QT_NAMESPACE_AS_OLD
    NEW_NAMESPACE "MyNS"
    CODESIGN
)
\endcode
*/
